Hi Jack,
   Fortran can use LIBOTS, which uses DecThreads.

Using Ladebug, extract the following information from the application/core.

pthread "system"             ! system info
pthread "versions"           ! DECthreads version
pthread "vp"             ! Brief listing of VPs
pthread "thread -1a"     ! Brief listing of threads
pthread "mutex -faql"    ! Full listing of all locked mutexes
pthread "cond -faqw"     ! Full listing of all CV's with waiters
pthread "rwlocks -faqr   ! Full listing of all read-locks
pthread "rwlocks -faqw   ! Full listing of all write-locks
pthread "show -tuvk"     ! Timeslice, Upcall, VP (on old versions), and 
kernel information
pthread "vp -f"          ! Full info on all VPs
pthread "thread -fa"     ! Full info on all threads
pthread "stacks -fs"     ! Display each threads' stacks beginning and 
end points.
pthread "mutex -faq"     ! Full info on all mutexes
pthread "cond -faq"      ! Full info on all CV's
pthread "rwlocks -faq    ! Full listing of all write-locks
pthread "vm -cf"         ! DECthreads internal memory manager
pthread "keys"           ! List of per-thread context keys
pthread "mutex -h"       ! History for mutexes, if available (typically 
not)
pthread "cond -h"        ! History for condition variables, if available 
(typically not)

  This will be a good starting point to determine the root cause of 
failure. I would be happy to assist if you prefer. Simply forward the 
information to me at:

reguera@zko.dec.com

thanks

John Reguera
Hewlett Packard
POSIX Threads Engineering.


