mark.bergman@thales-is.com (Mark Bergman) wrote in message news:<e9fa467e.0211130634.53ae4911@posting.google.com>...
> I have installed ssh on 2 Compaq (4.0F) machines.
> The machines only have the basic 4-user OSF-USR license.
> I ran ssh-keygen on one (client), copied the id_rsa.pub to the server
> in an authorized_keys file, and run sshd on the server.
> 
> When I run ssh on the client, I get an error message "Too many users"
> and cannot login.
> This message is usually given when 4 different user-ids are already
> logged-on, but I get it even if only 1 user-id is logged on.
> 
> I have checked that rlogin works in the same case, and that I can
> logon (using ssh) to the root account (the root account is not
> affected by the 4-user limit).
> I have also checked that my ssh client works to a different platform
> (IBM AIX) and that my server gives the same error when ssh is run on a
> different platform (Sun).
> 
> I have looked at the sshd debug - couldn't see anything obvious (I can
> post this if helpful)
> Does anyone know why the user-id limit is being imposed here?
> 
> TIA
> Mark Bergman
> Manchester UK
> mark . bergman @ thales-is.com

  
 The user-id limit is being imposed as a result of the license you 
bought (4-user OSF-USR). 
  To make ssh work you could put "UseLogin yes" in your sshd_config 
file on Compaq (4.0F) machine to make sshd use /bin/login. /bin/login
adheres to the license, and should allow you to have 4 users logged in
simultaneously.
  Note that when your license expires, it will tell you "Too many 
users are logged on" even if there are no users on the system.


Yuriy


