> 
>> Thanks to Hans S. who suggested I turn off Privilege Separation
>> (in sshd_config, "UsePrivilegeSeparation no").
> 
>> (Anyone know why this is necessary?)
> 
> Anyone know what "Privilege Separation" is??

I don't know the details, but most of the code of open-ssh was
rewritten rather recently to use priverlage separation. Most of the
code doesn't operate with any privelages, just the parts that need
it. That way, if a vulnerability is found, then it most likely only
affects the part of the code that is not operating under any
privelages.

There was a saga about 2 months ago, when this was written, because
the author knew there was a vulnerability, and that privelage
separation would "fix" it for the time-being, but didn't want to let
anyone know what the vulnerability was, otherwise you could end up
with a mass-virus very quickly. People feared that the author may be
up to something nasty, becauase of the secrecy though. 

Blame slashdot :)

