To the group:

I've been on the Tru64 Managers list for a while and the redhat axp
list.  This one looks pretty good .. a little more chatty than t64
managers.

i started using OSF1 in a casual way as an ArcInfo slave working for
the Navajo Nation in Window Rock.  Tha Alpha machines changed the way
GIS work got done - common ArcInfo operations on large, complex
"covers", like BUILD and CLEAN, that used to take long enough to get a
cup of coffee, all of a sudden completed in less than a minute.  A
whole new world of slavery!

Years later, I got called in to resurrect my old office's GIS system,
which i found vandalized, major parts stolen, the rest covered in
fine, red sand in a trailer in Tuba City on the western Rez.  It had
data on it that had cost us $250,000, and that could be used to save
whole families from losing their land.

I, who had devoutly leaqrned as little Unix as possible, just enough
to cd and ls, move around the system ... had to hack into a dead
system, nop logs to guide me, no manuals, not even certain the stuff
would do any more than light up its LEDs.

Fortunately, the old Digital sales and service guys were still around
and had maintained their contacts.  They gave me the basic info i
needed to hack the system - it has C-2 security - and I was able to
buy parts and stuff on the internet.

At this time, i am using a Dec pws500a that i set up for Tru64 using
the hobbyist kit, along with an unused ArcInfo license.  It took a
while but i got ArcInfo up and running, sorted everything out -- the
newer Alphas are awesome! Huge ArcInfo plot files load into a graphics
window in a blink of the eye!

The old Dec people were SO helpful, especially Dr. Blinn, Jay
Estabrook and Allen Rollow AKA Dr. Filesystem.

Anyway, the next thing is to set up Samba so I can serve the ArcInfo
coverages to a W2000 machine running ArcGIS.  And then get the old
3000/600 machine upgraded and running the same way. A guy in Russia
sez he will send be a copy of T64.0f and their OSF1 license should
still be good.

life is sweet.

jn

AKA "Thursday"


