I seem to have found what I need now.  After reading your post and
searching around in the inode headers for various filesystems, I
realized that st_flags reflects the di_flags member (if there is one)
of the dinode structure ( the on-disk portion of the inode ) for a
given file.   For ufs filesystems, the information I've been looking
for appears to be in /usr/include/ufs/dinode.h.  The flags are defined
as follows:

#define IC_FASTLINK     0x0001          /* Symbolic link in inode */
#define IC_PROPLIST_FRAG        0x0002
#define IC_PROPLIST_BLOCK       0x0004
#define IC_PROPLIST             0x0006  /* Property list exists */
#define IC_XUID                 0x0020  /* uid translation complete */
#define IC_XGID                 0x0040  /* gid translation complete */

I did a little testing to try & verify that these are valid, and it
looks like they are.  Symlinks typically have IC_FASTLINK set, while
files with extended file attributes ( including ACLs ) typically have
IC_PROPLIST and IC_PROPLIST_FRAG set.  IC_XUID and IC_XGID seem to
always be true on UFS.  It looks like AdvFS, System V fs, and nfs do
not use st_flags.  I'm probably safe ignoring cdfs & dvdfs for the
time being, and I suspect it'll turn out they don't have useful info
in st_flags either.

This is going to be useful information for me.  Being able to tell
quickly whether a file has extended attributes ( with the caveat that
this only works on UFS ) may end up giving me a significant
performance boost.  In contrast, Solaris 9 (for example) requires an
additional call to pathconf() to get the same information.

Thanks again,
  Vince


