I have been trying to find an answer to this, but haven't found any.

I have recently taken over the management of our Tru64 Unix systems,
currently running 4.0f, and have found out that there have been virtually no
backups performed on these systems since their installation.  I am new to
the Unix management scene, and from what I have been told, you cannot backup
the "/" partition while the systems is in multi-user mode and use this
backup to recover the system should the system disk fail.

My problem here is that these systems are in use 24x7x365 and cannot be
taken offline without causing problems for the company's operation.

My question is, is this correct?  Is there any way that a full backup of the
"/" and "/usr" partitions be performed while the system is in use, and be
able to recover the system using these backups in the event of a failure.

I will be replacing, and upgrading these systems soon to 5.1A,  Is there a
way to perform live backups available in this version that are not in 4.0f?

Note that the current systems are using UFS, not ADFS as the file system.  I
am not yet sure of what options I have for the File System on the new
upgraded systems,, this will be dictated by the primary application.  If I
need a specific file system to perform this sort of backup, then this is the
time for me to find out, as I may be able to influence the set-up of the new
systems.

Thanks in advance for any assistance
Shawn Kearley




