In article <5UPy9.6258$i%.1576806@localhost>,
Shawn Kearley <skearley@newfoundlandpower.com> wrote:
> I have recently taken over the management of our Tru64 Unix systems,
> currently running 4.0f, and have found out that there have been virtually no
> backups performed on these systems since their installation.  I am new to
> the Unix management scene, and from what I have been told, you cannot backup
> the "/" partition while the systems is in multi-user mode and use this
> backup to recover the system should the system disk fail.

That's not actually true. This is not Windows: files are not locked from
being backed up just because they're in use, and there are no system files
that I know of that have any significant chance of being left in an incomplete
state by a backup.

We use AMANDA, the free backup package from the University of Maryland, to
back up our Tru64-based network and the Xenix-based network it replaced for
something like a decade now. It's reliable, efficient, and robust.

> I will be replacing, and upgrading these systems soon to 5.1A,  Is there a
> way to perform live backups available in this version that are not in 4.0f?

5.1a uses ADVFS, which is possibly a little more fragile than UFS (at least
I have had more problems with it), but we use AMANDA there too.

