Hi, I'm not sure if this is right forum for this, but here goes:

I have written a java console application, to be run on Tru64, designed to 
stress-test one of  our applications. It can potentially spawn a lot of 
threads - on my Linux workstation I was getting OutOfMemoryErrors. After an 
ugly workaround (see hackish code below) it runs quite well [on my 
workstation]. Sooo... transferred the app over to the test Tru64 box. At 
the really stressful part of the app (spawning a lot of threads) the VM 
abruptly dies, with a range of console errors such as:

htable_insert: out of memory, file /util/htable.c, line 276
or
pool_alloc: out of memory, file /util/pool.c, line 117
or
array_enlarge: out of memory, file /util/sarray.c, line 37

...and sometimes it runs fine. Not often though!

The section of code that I think is bombing is

    public void sendTransaction()
    {
        boolean spawned = false;
        while(!spawned)
        {
            try
            {
                transactionThread tx = new transactionThread(this);
                tx.start();
                spawned = true;
            }
            catch(OutOfMemoryError oe)
            {
                try
                {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                }
                catch(InterruptedException e)
                {}
            }
            catch(Throwable t)
            {
                System.err.println("sendTransaction: caught an exception");
                t.printStackTrace(System.err);
                System.exit(1);
            }
        }

It's entirely possible for this function to be executed 1000 times or so in 
a row, resulting in a lot of threads... to the point where the VM on my 
Linux box gave up (gracefully), hence the try and catch for 
OutOfMemoryError. I kind of expected that Tru64 might also have trouble 
spawning that many threads so rapidly, but expected that OutOfMemoryError 
conditions would be caught by existing code.

Anyone got any ideas on this? It looks to me like a problem with the VM 
implementation itself. I'd like to try an earler VM (1.3.x) but since this 
app includes 1.4 specific code, I can't unless I rewrite it. 

Other workarounds might be to manually limit (in Java) the number of allowed 
threads, but I would prefer to avoid this, as it would necessarily be 
arbitrary based on our Alpha's setup and some trial and error.

I also tried some playing around with thread stack size with -Xssn, but I 
can't find a lot of doco about it (for example, what are the units? How 
should the required amount be calculated? etc) The app did not bomb out 
when I manually set -Xss, but it ran once, then never finished its second 
run. It seemed to have hung badly. Since I had no real idea what to do with 
this option, I stopped.

System details (mail me if you need more details)

$ sizer -v
Digital UNIX V4.0F  (Rev. 1229)

$ java -version
java version "1.4.0"
Java(TM) 2 Runtime Environment, Standard Edition
Fast VM (build 1.4.0-1, native threads, mixed mode, precompiled rt.jar, 
09/05/2002-19:05)


Hoping someone out there might be using the same Java version on Tru64 and 
be able to help.

Regards, Philip Yarra.

