tried to compile PGP (5.0) I spent two days cleaning up the source. Namely, GCC 
supports one feature that is not in any of the C language standards (except GCC 
standard) - indented CPP directives.

While it may look nice to have indented "#include" and "#ifdef" stuff in your 
code and does inhance readability, it is totally unsupported by DEC CC. ANSI C 
(and DEC CC) supports "semi-indentation": "#" must be in the first column, while 
the remaining text may be indented, like this:

#ifdef INT64
#  define HAS_INT64
#endif

Also, one should cleanup the configure script(s), too, since these things can 
lead to false detection of features NOT present on the system or the other way 
around.

Personally, PGP pissed me off sufficient number of times, so I switched to GPG - 
GNU PG, which is functionally equivalent. http://www.gnupg.org/

Nixie.



