In article <4NOV02.03160965@psfc.mit.edu>, mrl@psfc.mit.edu (Mark London) writes:
|> Can anyone tell me why it is that in Netscape, the displayed graphic images
|> seem to have low resolution?  Is a way to fix this?  Is there a version of
|> Mozilla that works under tru64?  Thanks.

I would guess that you have a 256-color display and that the display
manager and other applications are eating up so many entries in the
default color map that there are few left for your browser.  Here are
some things you could try:

1) If your graphics chip doesn't support a more than an 8-bit color
depth, replace it with one that does.

2) If your chip does support a greater color depth but is not using
it add "-vclass TrueColor" to the :0 line in /var/X11/xdm/Xservers
or /etc/dt/config/Xservers, depending on which display manager you
use.

3) Have Netscape use it's own colormap using the "-install" flag.
However, netscape will look wonky when you focus elsewhere and
everything else will look wonky when you focus on Netscape.

4) Try color management.  Make use of ~/.Xdefaults, color-related
application flags, and other mechanisms to have them use as many
of the same colors as possible, leaving more for your browser.

							- DDH






