look at a possible HP/Encompass point of view for a moment.

The conference, like all other conferences was held to enable certain
messages to be delivered, techniques to be discussed, and as an opportunity
for face-to-face discussions. The conference cost each participant money to
defray the expense of holding it.

Like other conferences held previously by other vendors and organizations,
I'm sure that there were the usual trinkets (I wasn't there) such as canvas
literature bags with logos, pens with logos, 'nerf' footballs with logos,
peppermint candies wrapped within logo-encrusted foil, and all the other
customary business-decision making necessities (also logo encrusted). As
part of this 'exclusive' environment, the organizers have decided that the
information that formed the heart of the presented part of the conference
should be reserved for the exclusive use of the exclusive list of attendees,
just in the same way that the conference-exclusive peppermint candies
wrapped within logo-encrusted foil are also not being sent to the great
unwashed who didn't pony up the money to attend.

We are talking in some cases about some information that was given under
NDA's. Even though the information disclosed under the NDA will not help
HP's market position vis-a-vis VMS one iota given that HP refuses to market
VMS, it none the less must remain exclusive to those who attended and paid
the money. That disclosing this information to the wide audience of people
who could not attend may improve the likelihood that HP gear, and OpenVMS in
particular, will not be unceremoniously dumped from hundreds, or thousands,
of commercial customer sites clearly is of no interest to HP.

Rest assured that some of this information will eventually make its way into
view...as part of an article in the Harvard Business Review, when they show
that had HP disclosed the information on a timely basis to a wide audience,
they would have been able to stem the erosion in their customer base that
ultimately lead to its bankruptcy.


Either all the foregoing, or HP really thinks that the money raised by the
CD sales can be booked in the 'VMS-related' category and will again bring
annual sales back to the $4B level.




